# JavaScript Fundamentals

## Variables
- `var` – function scoped, avoid
- `let` – block scoped, reassignable
- `const` – block scoped, not reassignable

## Data Types
- Primitives: string, number, boolean, null, undefined, symbol, bigint
- Objects: object, array, function

```js
let name = "Alice";
const age = 25;
let isStudent = true;
let arr = [1, 2, 3];
let obj = { a: 1, b: 2 };
```

## Operators
- Arithmetic: `+ - * / % **`
- Comparison: `== != === !== > < >= <=`
- Logical: `&& || !`
- Nullish: `??`
- Optional chaining: `?.`

## Control Flow
```js
if (score > 80) {
  grade = "A";
} else if (score > 60) {
  grade = "B";
} else {
  grade = "F";
}

for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) { ... }
for (const x of arr) { ... }
for (const k in obj) { ... }
while (cond) { ... }
```

## Edge Cases
- `==` performs type coercion; use `===`
- `NaN !== NaN`
- `typeof null === "object"` (historical bug)
- `const obj = {}; obj.x = 1` is allowed (only binding is constant)

## Interview FAQs
**Q1: `==` vs `===`?**
`===` compares type and value without coercion.

**Q2: `let` vs `var`?**
`let` is block scoped; `var` is function scoped and hoisted.

**Q3: What is hoisting?**
Declarations moved to top of scope at compile time.

# jQuery Fundamentals

## Why jQuery?
Concise syntax, cross-browser, easy DOM manipulation and AJAX. Still used in legacy projects.

## Include
```html
<script src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.7.1.min.js"></script>
```

## Selectors
```js
$("#id");
$(".class");
$("ul li");
$("input[type='text']");
```

## Manipulation
```js
$("#box").text("Hi");
$("#box").html("<b>Hi</b>");
$("#box").addClass("active");
$("#box").attr("data-id", 5);
$("#box").css("color", "red");
$("#box").append("<span>New</span>");
$("#box").remove();
```

## Events
```js
$("#btn").on("click", function () { ... });
$("#list").on("click", ".item", function () { ... }); // delegation
$("#btn").off("click");
```

## Effects
```js
$("#box").hide();
$("#box").show();
$("#box").fadeIn(300);
$("#box").slideToggle();
```

## Chaining
```js
$("#box").addClass("active").fadeIn(200).css("margin", "10px");
```

## Edge Cases
- `$(this)` inside handlers
- jQuery object is not an array but is array-like
- `.on()` is preferred over deprecated `.bind()` and `.click()` for dynamic elements

## Interview FAQs
**Q1: `$(document).ready()` vs `window.onload`?**
ready fires after DOM parsed; onload waits for all assets.

**Q2: How does jQuery delegation work?**
`$(parent).on(event, selector, handler)`.

**Q3: Is jQuery still relevant?**
For legacy codebases yes; for new apps modern frameworks or vanilla JS preferred.

# jQuery Traversal & Forms

## Traversal
```js
$("#item").parent();
$("#item").children();
$("#item").closest("tr");
$("#item").find(".price");
$("#item").siblings();
$("#item").next();
$("#item").prev();
```

## Iteration
```js
$(".row").each(function (i, el) {
  console.log(i, $(el).text());
});
```

## Forms
```js
$("#form").on("submit", function (e) {
  e.preventDefault();
  const data = $(this).serialize();
  const obj = $(this).serializeArray();
  console.log(data, obj);
});

$("#city").val();
$("#city").val("Delhi");
$("input[name='gender']:checked").val();
```

## Attributes & Data
```js
$el.attr("href");
$el.data("userId");    // reads data-user-id
$el.data("userId", 5);
```

## Edge Cases
- `.val()` returns string; cast for numbers
- `serialize()` skips disabled fields
- `data-*` attributes are cached after first read

## Interview FAQs
**Q1: attr vs prop?**
attr for HTML attributes; prop for live DOM properties (e.g., checked).

**Q2: How to read all form fields?**
`$(form).serialize()` or `serializeArray()`.

**Q3: How to get selected radio value?**
`$('input[name=gender]:checked').val()`.

# Fetch, Promises & Async/Await

## Fetch Basic
```js
fetch("/api/users")
  .then(res => {
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(res.status);
    return res.json();
  })
  .then(data => console.log(data))
  .catch(err => console.error(err));
```

## POST
```js
fetch("/api/users", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
  body: JSON.stringify({ name: "Alice" })
})
  .then(r => r.json())
  .then(console.log);
```

## Async/Await
```js
async function loadUsers() {
  try {
    const res = await fetch("/api/users");
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(res.status);
    const data = await res.json();
    console.log(data);
  } catch (e) {
    console.error(e);
  }
}
```

## Promise Combinators
```js
Promise.all([p1, p2, p3]);     // waits all, fails if any fails
Promise.allSettled([...]);     // resolves with all results
Promise.race([...]);           // first to finish
Promise.any([...]);            // first to succeed
```

## Edge Cases
- fetch does not reject on HTTP error (only network)
- Always check `res.ok`
- No default timeout – use AbortController
- Body can only be read once

## Interview FAQs
**Q1: Does fetch reject on 404?**
No – you must check `res.ok`.

**Q2: Promise vs callback?**
Promises avoid callback hell and support chaining.

**Q3: What is async/await?**
Syntactic sugar over promises for sequential async code.

# Dynamic HTML Tables

## Goal
Render tabular data from an API, add search, sort, pagination and CRUD.

## Minimal HTML
```html
<input id="search" placeholder="Search...">
<table class="table table-striped" id="usersTable">
  <thead>
    <tr><th data-key="id">ID</th><th data-key="name">Name</th><th>Actions</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody id="tbody"></tbody>
</table>
```

## Rendering Rows (Vanilla JS)
```js
function render(rows) {
  const tbody = document.getElementById("tbody");
  tbody.innerHTML = "";
  rows.forEach(r => {
    const tr = document.createElement("tr");
    tr.innerHTML = `
      <td>${r.id}</td>
      <td>${r.name}</td>
      <td><button class="btn btn-sm btn-danger delete" data-id="${r.id}">Delete</button></td>`;
    tbody.appendChild(tr);
  });
}
```

## Rendering Rows (jQuery)
```js
function render(rows) {
  const $tbody = $("#tbody").empty();
  rows.forEach(r => {
    $tbody.append(`
      <tr>
        <td>${r.id}</td>
        <td>${r.name}</td>
        <td><button class="btn btn-sm btn-danger delete" data-id="${r.id}">Delete</button></td>
      </tr>`);
  });
}
```

## Search Filter
```js
$("#search").on("input", function () {
  const q = $(this).val().toLowerCase();
  $("#tbody tr").filter(function () {
    $(this).toggle($(this).text().toLowerCase().includes(q));
  });
});
```

## Sort by Column
```js
$("th[data-key]").on("click", function () {
  const key = $(this).data("key");
  const dir = $(this).data("dir") === "asc" ? "desc" : "asc";
  $(this).data("dir", dir);
  data.sort((a, b) => dir === "asc" ? (a[key] > b[key] ? 1 : -1) : (a[key] < b[key] ? 1 : -1));
  render(data);
});
```

## Pagination (Client-side)
```js
let page = 1, perPage = 5;
function paginate(rows) {
  const start = (page - 1) * perPage;
  render(rows.slice(start, start + perPage));
}
```

## Delete Row with Delegation
```js
$("#tbody").on("click", ".delete", function () {
  const id = $(this).data("id");
  $(this).closest("tr").remove();
  // optionally call DELETE API
});
```

## Edge Cases
- Escape HTML to prevent XSS
- Do not rebind events for each render – use delegation
- Large datasets: paginate server-side
- Preserve state (page, sort) after re-render

## Interview FAQs
**Q1: How to avoid XSS when building rows from data?**
Use textContent or escape user strings.

**Q2: Why is delegation best for dynamic tables?**
Handles newly added rows without rebinding.

**Q3: Client vs server pagination?**
Client good for small data; server for large.

# CRUD Table with AJAX – Complete Project

## Goal
Build a user management table using Bootstrap + jQuery + AJAX.

## Features
1. List users from API
2. Add user (modal)
3. Edit user (modal)
4. Delete user (confirm)
5. Search + sort + pagination
6. Toasts for feedback
7. Loading spinner

## Folder Structure
```
project/
├── index.html
├── js/app.js
├── js/api.js
└── css/style.css
```

## api.js
```js
const API = {
  base: "https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com",
  getUsers: () => $.get(`${API.base}/users`),
  createUser: (u) => $.ajax({ url: `${API.base}/users`, method: "POST", contentType: "application/json", data: JSON.stringify(u) }),
  updateUser: (id, u) => $.ajax({ url: `${API.base}/users/${id}`, method: "PUT", contentType: "application/json", data: JSON.stringify(u) }),
  deleteUser: (id) => $.ajax({ url: `${API.base}/users/${id}`, method: "DELETE" })
};
```

## app.js
```js
let users = [];

$(document).ready(async function () {
  await load();
});

async function load() {
  $("#loading").show();
  users = await API.getUsers();
  render(users);
  $("#loading").hide();
}

function render(rows) {
  const $tbody = $("#tbody").empty();
  rows.forEach(u => $tbody.append(rowHtml(u)));
}

function rowHtml(u) {
  return `<tr>
    <td>${u.id}</td>
    <td>${u.name}</td>
    <td>${u.email}</td>
    <td>
      <button class="btn btn-sm btn-warning edit" data-id="${u.id}">Edit</button>
      <button class="btn btn-sm btn-danger delete" data-id="${u.id}">Delete</button>
    </td>
  </tr>`;
}

$("#tbody").on("click", ".delete", async function () {
  const id = $(this).data("id");
  if (!confirm("Delete this user?")) return;
  await API.deleteUser(id);
  users = users.filter(u => u.id !== id);
  render(users);
  toast("User deleted");
});

$("#saveUser").on("click", async function () {
  const name = $("#name").val().trim();
  const email = $("#email").val().trim();
  if (!name || !email) return toast("Fill all fields");
  await API.createUser({ name, email });
  toast("User added");
  bootstrap.Modal.getInstance($("#userModal")[0]).hide();
  load();
});

function toast(msg) {
  const id = "t" + Date.now();
  $("#toasts").append(`<div id="${id}" class="toast show"><div class="toast-body">${msg}</div></div>`);
  setTimeout(() => $("#" + id).remove(), 3000);
}
```

## Edge Cases
- Always show loading state
- Confirm destructive actions
- Validate before sending
- Handle API failure with try/catch
- Avoid XSS by escaping user input in HTML

## Interview FAQs
**Q1: How to structure API calls?**
Separate API module, keep UI logic in app.

**Q2: How to make it optimistic?**
Update UI immediately, roll back on failure.

**Q3: How to test?**
Manual test with network tab and API mocking (Postman / MSW).
